 },
 "requested_at": "2026-11-13T20:08:54.157397Z",
 "text": "Under the *Residential Tenancies Act, 2006* (RTA), a landlord cannot charge or collect fees, penalties, or additional amounts beyond lawful rent and an authorized rent deposit. \n\nOnce a tenancy is lawfully terminated, the tenant's obligation to pay rent ends. Requiring a tenant to pay rent for a period after the tenancy has ended constitutes an illegal penalty or prohibited charge against a former tenant under section 134 of the Act. Under subsection 134(1)(a), a landlord is prohibited from requiring or collecting such amounts from a tenant or former tenant, \"even if the amount is also payable under an agreement between the landlord and tenant.\" Consequently, such a clause is void and unenforceable (also reinforced generally by section 4).\n\nVerdict: not enforceable\nSection: 134",
